          A typical dishwasher lasts about 9 to 10 years, and most land somewhere between 9 and 12 years before a repair stops making sense. Hard water, a never-cleaned filter and heavy daily use push that toward the low end; soft water, a clean filter and a household that runs it a few times a week push it toward the high end. The machine rarely dies all at once. It fails one part at a time, and the real question is when the next part stops being worth fixing.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Long Does a Dishwasher Actually Last?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The number most often quoted comes from the National Association of Home Builders' study of home component life expectancy, which put the dishwasher at about nine years. Appliance makers and repair trades tend to quote a slightly wider band of roughly 9 to 12 years. Both describe the same reality: a dishwasher is one of the shorter-lived major appliances in a kitchen, well behind a range or a refrigerator, because it combines hot water, detergent chemistry, a pump, a heater and a door that opens and closes thousands of times.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do the arithmetic and the decade makes sense. A household that runs one load a day puts roughly 3,650 cycles on a machine in ten years. Each cycle fills the tub, heats water, sprays it at pressure through rotating arms, drains, refills, and often runs a heated dry. Seals swell and shrink with every cycle, the door hinges carry the weight of a loaded rack, and the pump moves gritty, greasy water every single time. Wear is the expected outcome, not a defect.
          </p>

          <p>
            Usage matters more than the calendar. A two-person household that runs the dishwasher three times a week may put fewer than half as many cycles on it as a family of five running it daily. That is why one neighbor's machine dies at seven years while another's soldiers on at fifteen. If you want a better estimate for your own machine, think in cycles and water quality, not birthdays.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Lifespan in Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~9 yrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">NAHB life expectancy figure</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">9-12 yrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Common industry range</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~3,650</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Cycles in 10 years at one load a day</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">50%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Repair-cost rule of thumb</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Price tier changes the number less than people expect. Expensive machines usually bring a stainless tub, quieter insulation, better racks and more features, and the stainless tub in particular resists staining and cracking better than a plastic one. But the parts that typically end a dishwasher's life, the pump, the control board and the door latch, are similar in principle across price points. A premium machine with an expensive electronic failure at year eight can be a worse repair decision than a budget machine with a cheap one.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the honest answer is this: plan for about ten years, be pleasantly surprised by twelve or more, and do not be shocked by seven if you have hard water and a busy kitchen. Everything below is about which side of that range you land on, how to tell where you are now, and what to do when something breaks.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-fails" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Usually Breaks First?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Dishwashers fail in fairly predictable places. The drain pump, the circulation or wash motor, the electronic control board, the door latch assembly and the heating element account for most of the calls that end in a replace-or-repair conversation. Smaller parts such as the water inlet valve, the float switch, the door gasket and the hinge springs or cables fail too, but those are usually cheap enough that nobody replaces a whole machine over them.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The pumps and motor</h3>

          <p>
            Most machines have two pumping jobs: a circulation pump that sprays water through the arms and a drain pump that empties the tub. The drain pump is the more common failure, and it often starts as a symptom that looks like a clog: standing water in the bottom after a cycle, a humming sound with no draining, or a grinding noise from a bit of glass or a fruit pit lodged in the impeller. Sometimes clearing the debris fixes it. If the motor itself is burned out, it is a parts replacement, and on many models the circulation motor is one of the more expensive parts in the machine.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The control board</h3>

          <p>
            The control board is the dishwasher's brain, and it is the failure that most often pushes an older machine into the replace column. Symptoms include buttons that stop responding, cycles that stop partway, lights that flash an error code, or a machine that is completely dead even though the breaker is fine. Moisture that gets past a door seal, a failing touchpad and power surges are common culprits. Boards are not cheap, and diagnosing whether the board or a sensor is actually at fault takes time, which adds labor.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The door latch and heating element</h3>

          <p>
            A door latch or door switch failure is one of the most common reasons a machine will not start at all, because the dishwasher will not run unless it senses the door is closed. It is usually an inexpensive part and a reasonable repair. The heating element, the exposed loop in the bottom of many tubs, can burn out and leave you with dishes that are wet, cool or poorly cleaned. Many European-style machines heat water in a flow-through heater built into the pump assembly instead, which changes the part but not the symptom.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
