              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="what-shortens" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Shortens a Dishwasher's Life?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Hard water is the biggest silent factor. The U.S. Geological Survey classifies water above about 121 milligrams per liter of calcium carbonate as hard and above 180 as very hard, and a large share of the country sits in those ranges. Every cycle leaves a little mineral scale on the heating element, inside spray arm nozzles, on the inlet valve screen and in the pump. Over years that scale makes the heater work harder, starves the spray arms of pressure and gives the pump more to fight against.
          </p>

          <p>
            The detergent change of 2010 made this worse for many households. Around that time the industry removed phosphates from automatic dishwasher detergent, largely in response to state bans on phosphates in that product. Phosphates were very good at keeping minerals in suspension, so after the switch a lot of people in hard-water areas started seeing cloudy glasses, white film and more scale inside the machine. Rinse aid and a detergent formulated for hard water help close that gap.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second factor is the filter. Most dishwashers sold in the last decade or so use a manual filter in the bottom of the tub rather than a self-cleaning hard-food disposer, because filters are quieter and help machines meet efficiency targets. The trade-off is that someone has to take the filter out and rinse it. When nobody does, food debris and grease build up, the drain pump strains, water drains slowly, odors develop, and the wash gets worse, which tempts people into running extra cycles that add more wear.
          </p>

          <p>
            Installation and loading matter too. A drain hose without a high loop or an air gap can let sink water siphon back into the tub. A machine that is not level can leak at the door or let the door gasket wear unevenly. Overloading blocks spray arms and puts weight on the racks and hinges, and loose items like bottle caps and broken glass end up in the pump. None of these will kill a machine in a month, but each one quietly moves it toward the short end of the range.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, there is how often you run it. Running a dishwasher full and once a day is far kinder than running it half-empty twice a day, because cycles, not hours, are what wear the pump, the latch and the door hinges. If your household is small, letting dishes accumulate for a full load is both more efficient and better for the machine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="signs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How Can You Tell a Dishwasher Is Dying?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Some signs mean a single part is failing; others mean the whole machine is near the end. The single-part signs include water left in the bottom after a cycle, dishes that come out cold and wet, a machine that will not start, or a new grinding or humming noise. Those are worth diagnosing before you give up. The whole-machine signs are different: rust inside the tub or on the racks that has worked through the coating, water on the floor from a cracked tub, or a door that will no longer seal even with a new gasket.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch for patterns. One repair in eight years is normal. Two or three repairs within a year or so on a machine past its seventh birthday usually means the rest of the parts are reaching the same age. The pump, the board and the latch were all installed the same day and have all run the same number of cycles, so when one fails late in life, the others are rarely far behind.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">STOP USING IT</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A burning smell or tripped breaker is not a wait-and-see problem.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If you smell hot plastic or electrical burning, or the dishwasher keeps tripping its breaker or GFCI, shut it off at the breaker and do not run it again until it has been inspected. Wiring and control board faults inside a cabinet are a fire risk, not just an inconvenience.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Leaks deserve special attention because they do damage beyond the appliance. Water under a dishwasher can soak into the subfloor and cabinet base for weeks before you notice warped flooring or a musty smell. If you see water at the front edge of the kickplate, check the door gasket and the level first. If the leak is coming from under the machine, the culprit may be a pump seal, a loose hose clamp, or a cracked tub, and the last one is not worth repairing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Declining wash quality is the subtlest sign. If dishes that used to come out clean now come out with grit, film or food stuck on, start with the cheap checks: clean the filter, clear the spray arm holes, run a cleaning cycle with a citric acid based cleaner, and make sure your hot water reaches the kitchen hot. If none of that helps, a weak circulation motor or a failing heater is likely, and that is where age starts to drive the decision.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="repair-replace" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Should You Repair or Replace It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The rule of thumb used across the appliance trade is the 50 percent rule: if the repair costs more than about half the price of a comparable new machine and the dishwasher is past roughly half its expected life, replace it. For a dishwasher, half its life is around year five. So a cheap door latch on a nine-year-old machine is fine to fix, but a control board plus labor on that same machine usually is not.
          </p>

          <p>
            Labor is what changes the math. In many areas, a service call fee alone runs around a hundred dollars or more before any parts. That means even a modest repair can approach a third of the cost of an entry-level dishwasher once the diagnosis, part and labor are added up. If you are comfortable with basic appliance work, the picture changes: door latches, inlet valves, door gaskets, spray arms and many drain pumps are parts a handy owner can replace with a screwdriver and the model number.
          </p>

          <p>
            Before you decide, look up the model number, which is usually printed on a label on the edge of the door or the tub opening. Search for the specific part and error code. Some models have well-known weak points with inexpensive fixes, and some have a reputation for a board failure that is expensive every time. Ten minutes of research can tell you whether you are looking at a one-off or the first of several failures.
          </p>

          <p>
            Efficiency is rarely a strong reason to replace a working machine on its own. A newer dishwasher may use less water and energy than one from a decade ago, but the savings per year are modest, and a dishwasher that still cleans well is not costing you much to keep. The better reasons to replace are reliability, noise, cleaning performance and a tub or door that is physically wearing out.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you do replace it, buy for the problems that ended the old one. If hard water was the issue, look at machines with good filtration and plan for rinse aid. If the old one was loud, pay attention to decibel ratings. Our <a href="/best-dishwasher/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best dishwashers</a> guide sorts current models by what actually matters day to day, including how easy the filter is to clean.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="make-it-last" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Do You Make a Dishwasher Last Longer?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Clean the filter. It is the single most useful habit. Check your manual, but most manufacturers suggest rinsing it monthly, and more often if you do not scrape plates. Twist it out, rinse it under hot water, scrub the fine mesh gently with a soft brush, and put it back properly seated. It takes two minutes and it protects the drain pump, the wash quality and your nose.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fight scale before you can see it. If you have hard water, use rinse aid every load and run a dishwasher cleaner or a citric acid cleaning cycle every month or two. Wipe the door gasket and the bottom edge of the door, where food and detergent residue collect. Pull the spray arms off a few times a year and clear any blocked holes with a toothpick. If your water is very hard, a whole-house solution protects the dishwasher along with your water heater and fixtures; our guide to <a href="/best-water-softener-system/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">water softener systems</a> covers when that is worth the cost.
          </p>

          <p>
            Run the kitchen tap until the water is hot before you start a cycle. Many dishwashers rely on the incoming water temperature and then heat it further. Starting with lukewarm water makes the heater work harder and longer, and on some machines it hurts cleaning. It is a small habit that reduces load on one of the parts most likely to fail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Load it the way it was designed. Keep tall items from blocking the spray arms, do not overload the racks, and keep small loose items such as lids and caps in a basket where they cannot drop into the sump. Scrape off bones, seeds and large food, but you do not need to pre-rinse; modern detergents work better with some food soil to act on, and pre-rinsing just wastes water.
          </p>

          <p>
            Protect the electronics. A dishwasher on a shared kitchen circuit sees every spike from other appliances, and storms can damage control boards. Point-of-use protection is awkward for a hardwired dishwasher, but a <a href="/best-whole-house-surge-protector/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">whole-house surge protector</a> covers every board in the house at once. The same logic applies to every appliance with a lifespan question, which is why our guide to <a href="/how-long-do-garage-door-openers-last/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how long garage door openers last</a> spends time on logic boards too.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="when-changes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. When Does the Answer Change?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The ten-year answer assumes normal household use, municipal or treated water, and a machine installed correctly. Change any of those and the answer moves. Very hard well water without treatment can shorten the practical life of a heater and pump noticeably. A vacation home dishwasher run a few dozen times a year can last far longer than the average, though seals may dry out if it sits unused for long stretches.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rentals and busy family kitchens tend to sit at the bottom of the range. More cycles, less filter cleaning and more broken glass in the sump all add up. Landlords often plan dishwasher replacement on a shorter schedule than homeowners for exactly that reason, and they frequently buy simpler machines with fewer electronics because they are easier and cheaper to repair.
          </p>

          <p>
            Features also cut both ways. Wi-Fi connectivity, third racks, specialized wash zones and auto-open drying are useful, but each adds parts that can fail. A machine with a simple mechanical design and a good stainless tub can outlive a feature-packed one, not because the features are bad, but because there are fewer things to go wrong. Decide which features you will actually use before paying for them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, the warranty sets a floor, not a promise. Most dishwashers come with a one-year full warranty, with longer coverage on certain parts such as the tub, the racks or the control electronics depending on the brand. Read the fine print on what is covered for parts versus labor in later years. An extended warranty is rarely worth it on an appliance where the typical expensive repair arrives after the coverage ends.
          </p>

          <p>
            The same logic applies across the house. Water heaters, softeners, mini splits and sump pumps all have lifespans that are better described as ranges driven by water, use and maintenance than as a single number. If your dishwasher is near the end, it is worth checking the age of the others too; our guide to <a href="/how-long-do-tankless-water-heaters-last/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how long tankless water heaters last</a> is a good place to start.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Never cleaning the filter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most current dishwashers have a manual filter, not a grinder. Leaving it for months makes the drain pump work harder, leaves grit on dishes and causes odors. Rinsing it monthly takes two minutes and protects the part most likely to fail first. If you have never removed it, do it today before blaming the machine.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running extra cycles to fix bad cleaning</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                When dishes come out dirty, people often rewash them or run heavier cycles. That adds wear without fixing the cause. Clean the filter, clear the spray arm holes, check that the hot water is hot, and add rinse aid first. Extra cycles on a machine with a scale problem only bake the scale on harder.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying for a board on an old machine</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A control board plus diagnosis and labor on a machine past year seven or eight often costs close to half the price of a new dishwasher. The remaining parts are just as old and just as likely to fail next. Unless the board is cheap and you can fit it yourself, that money usually belongs toward a replacement.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring a slow leak</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A small leak under a dishwasher can soak the subfloor and cabinet base for weeks before anyone sees damage. If you notice water at the kickplate, a warped floor or a musty smell, pull the kickplate and look with a flashlight. Fixing a hose clamp now is far cheaper than replacing flooring later.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping rinse aid in hard water</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Since phosphates left dishwasher detergent around 2010, hard-water households have relied on rinse aid and good detergent to control film and scale. Skipping it means more mineral buildup on the heater, spray arms and pump. If your glasses are cloudy, the inside of the machine is getting the same treatment where you cannot see it.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it worth repairing a 10-year-old dishwasher?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually only for a cheap repair. A door latch, inlet valve or gasket on a ten-year-old machine can be worth doing, especially if you fit it yourself. A control board, circulation motor or anything involving a cracked tub is generally not, because the machine is already at the average lifespan and the other original parts are likely to fail soon after.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do more expensive dishwashers last longer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Somewhat, but less than the price gap suggests. Premium machines often have stainless tubs, better racks and sturdier hinges, which help. But the parts that usually end a dishwasher's life, the pump, control board and latch, fail across all price tiers. A premium machine can also cost more to repair, which can push an older one into replacement sooner.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should I clean my dishwasher filter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Check your manual, but monthly is a sensible default for most households, and every week or two if you load plates without scraping or run it daily. Remove the filter, rinse it under hot water, brush the mesh gently, and seat it back exactly as it came out. A filter that is not locked in place can let debris reach the pump.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does hard water really shorten dishwasher life?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It does. Mineral scale builds on the heating element, spray arms, inlet valve screen and pump, making each work harder and reducing cleaning performance. The effect is gradual rather than sudden, but it is one of the main reasons machines in hard-water areas fail earlier. Rinse aid, a monthly cleaning cycle and, for very hard water, a softener all help.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my dishwasher leave water in the bottom?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A little moisture around the sump is normal on many models. A pool of standing water usually means a clogged filter, a kinked or blocked drain hose, a clogged garbage disposal connection or a failing drain pump. Clean the filter and check the hose and disposal knockout first. If it still will not drain and you hear humming, the pump is the likely cause.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it bad to run a dishwasher every day?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Running it daily is normal and is exactly what it is built for. What matters is running it full. Two half loads put twice the cycles on the pump, latch and heater for the same number of dishes. If you can fill it once a day, running it daily is perfectly reasonable and more efficient than handwashing for most households.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a dishwasher warranty last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most manufacturers offer one year of full coverage on parts and labor, with longer coverage on specific components such as the tub, racks or certain electronic parts, depending on the brand. That extended part coverage often excludes labor. Read the terms before buying an extended warranty, since the expensive failures typically arrive after the coverage period has ended.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

