        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Plan on about ten years from a dishwasher, and treat anything past twelve as a bonus. The machine that gets there is usually the one with a clean filter, rinse aid in the dispenser, reasonably soft water and an owner who fixes the cheap failures promptly while letting the expensive ones on an old machine trigger a replacement.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            When a repair quote lands, compare it to half the price of a new machine and look at the age. Under five years, fix almost anything. Past eight, fix only the cheap parts. In between, look up the model's known weak points and decide with your eyes open.
          </p>
